DLC – Defect Life Cycle
DLC stands for Defect Life Cycle.
The alternative names of DLC are:
Bug Life Cycle
Status of Defect
Status of Bugs
When we are executing manual test cases, if the Expected Result and Actual Result are not the same, that is a defect.
Then the defect is raised to the developers. The status will be New.
The defect will be assigned to the concerned Developer or Development Lead. The status is Assigned.
Then the developer will reproduce the defect, and it will be given the status Reproducible. Then it will be accepted.
Developers start fixing the defect in the Development Server, and it will be installed in the Testing Server.
Then developers will change the status to Fixed.
Now Test Engineers will start retesting the defect in the Testing Server.
If the defect is properly fixed, then the status will be Closed.
If the defect is not fixed properly, then again the defect will be Reopened to the developers.
This process will continue.
Once the defect is closed, the status will be Pass.
This complete process is called Defect Life Cycle or Bug Life Cycle.
Other stages in Defect Life Cycle:
Duplicate
Deferred
Invalid
Can't Fix
Can't Reproduce
Request for Enhancement (RFE)
1. Duplicate
Whenever the same defect is raised multiple times, then the second defect will be changed to Duplicate status by the developers.
It is a Test Engineer's mistake.
Flowchart:
New / Open → Assign → Duplicate → Closed
It is a Developer's mistake:
New / Open → Assign → Duplicate → Assign → Fixed → Retest → Closed
How to avoid Duplicate status?
Communicate with the team members before raising the defect.
Check the repository before raising the defects.
The repository can be:
QC
ALM
Jira
Bugzilla
Note: Duplicate is a bad status for Test Engineers.
2. Deferred Status
Whenever Test Engineers raise the defect, developers have less time to fix the defect.
In this case, developers will postpone fixing minor defects.
Then they will change the status to Deferred or Postponed.
Flowchart:
New / Open → Assign → Deferred → Fixed → Retest → Closed
Note: Deferred is good for Test Engineers.
3. Invalid or Rejected Status
Whenever Test Engineers raise a defect to the developers, if developers are not accepting it as a defect, then it will be changed to Invalid status.
Example: If Developers are correct:
New / Open → Assign → Invalid → Closed
If Test Engineers are correct:
New / Open → Assign → Invalid → Assign → Fixed → Retest → Closed
4. Can't Reproduce
Whenever Test Engineers raise the defect and they also have proof, but developers are unable to see the defect.
In this case, they change the status to Can't Reproduce.
Flowchart:
New / Open → Assign → Can't Reproduce → Assign → Fixed → Retest → Closed
Reasons for Can't Reproduce:
Because of inconsistent bugs.
Due to installation problems.
Because of improper defect reports.
5. Can't Fix
Whenever Test Engineers raise the defect, but developers are unable to fix it, then they will change the status to Can't Fix.
Flowchart:
New / Open → Assign → Can't Fix → Closed
If it is a valid reason, it will be informed to the Manager or Business Analyst or Customer.
Once it is finalized, it will be closed.
Reasons for Can't Fix:
Technology problem.
Fixing one defect may lead to many defects.
6. Request for Enhancement (RFE)
Note: Request for Enhancement is a very important topic for getting a job in Cyberwell.
Analyzing the status is very important.
We can use tools like:
QC
ALM
Jira
Bugzilla
FireFlink
Etc.
The status of defects would be used in a defect report.
Whenever Test Engineers raise the defect, developers don't accept it as a defect, but developers accept it as a good suggestion.
In this case, developers will change the status to Request for Enhancement.
Flowchart:
New / Open → Assign → RFE
This whole process is called Status of Bug or Bug Life Cycle or Defect Life Cycle.
